# Security

- Never log, print, or include tokens in error messages (`sprites.token`, `modal.token_id`, `modal.token_secret`, `e2b.api_key`, `docker.pat`, `daytona.api_key`). Docker access tokens and the endpoint credentials of a sandbox count as tokens too; the docker package redacts them, only sends them to HTTPS endpoints (or loopback in tests), and never uses the PAT itself as a bearer token; its sandboxes have no reachable port unless `--api` publishes one, and the URL Docker returns is open to anyone who has it. E2B daemon access tokens count as tokens too; the e2b package redacts them, and sandbox URLs are private unless `--api` opens the API port. The daytona package redacts the API key from errors, and its preview URL is open to anyone who has it; `--api` makes the whole sandbox public. The blaxel package redacts the API key from errors, sends it only to `https://api.blaxel.ai` and to HTTPS hosts ending in `.bl.run`, and does not read `BL_API_KEY`, `BL_WORKSPACE`, or `~/.blaxel`. Its preview URL is open to anyone who has it.
- Do not execute user input via shell (`sh -c`); when exec is implemented, pass argv slices only.
- Store secrets in environment variables only; avoid long-lived secrets in command-line flags.
- Validate provider names before use.
